From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ole32, winex11: an OLE drag over another X program's window speaks XDND to it

A file dragged from File Explorer onto a Linux program (Kdenlive's project
bin, David 2026-11-15) went nowhere: Wine took XDND drops but never gave
any. DoDragDrop only knew Wine's own drop targets, and over a Linux window
WindowFromPoint names the Wine window under it (the taskbar's frame around
the program, the desktop), which may even take the drop.

Now DoDragDrop asks the X11 driver at every step (a display escape,
wine/sg_drag.h). The X windows under the pointer are walked from the root:
a window marked as Wine's (any Wine process, _SG_WINE_WINDOW; the virtual
desktop) leaves the drag to OLE, unless one further down belongs to another
program that speaks XDND (a framed Linux program). That program gets
XdndEnter, XdndPosition (the action the keys ask for), XdndLeave, XdndDrop
from a small source window that owns XdndSelection and converts the data
rendered once from the IDataObject: CF_HDROP as text/uri-list (file:// URIs,
links resolved, "/" unescaped as GLib needs), text as UTF-8, HTML, PNG, a DIB.
XdndStatus is the drag's effect, XdndFinished's action DoDragDrop's. A move
of files that are all still in place (the program kept a reference) is
reported as a copy, so the source deletes nothing nobody moved, unless the
program asked for DELETE. The IDropSource still decides go on, cancel, drop.
---
