new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5fca9a3
--- /dev/null
+++ b/dlls/winex11.drv/sg_dragsrc.c
@@ -0,0 +1,601 @@
+/*
+ * Stained Glass OS: Wine as an XDND source (wine-sg 7787)
+ *
+ * Wine took drops from other X programs (XDND target, event.c) but gave
+ * none: a file dragged from File Explorer onto a Linux program -- Kdenlive's
+ * project bin (David 2026-11-15) -- went nowhere, because ole32's DoDragDrop
+ * only knows Wine's own drop targets, and over a Linux window WindowFromPoint
+ * names whatever Wine window lies under it (the taskbar's frame around the
+ * Linux window, the desktop).
+ *
+ * Now ole32 asks here at every step of the drag (wine/sg_drag.h). The X
+ * windows under the pointer are walked from the root down: one marked as a
+ * Wine window (any Wine process, _SG_WINE_WINDOW; the virtual desktop) leaves
+ * the drag to OLE, unless a window further down is another program's that
+ * speaks XDND (a Linux program in the taskbar's frame); that program then
+ * gets the XDND messages from this thread's own small source window, which
+ * owns XdndSelection and converts the data ole32 rendered from the
+ * IDataObject when the drag first reached an X program: a file list as
+ * text/uri-list (file:// URIs of the Unix paths), text as UTF-8 and Latin-1,
+ * HTML, PNG, a DIB -- as the clipboard does. What the program accepts
+ * (XdndStatus) is the drag's effect; XdndFinished's action is DoDragDrop's.
+ *
+ * A move is the source deleting its data. An X program finishing with "move"
+ * on files that are all still where they were -- it took a reference, or it
+ * moves them later -- is reported as a copy, so that File Explorer does not
+ * delete files nobody moved; a program that asked for DELETE (the XDND way of
+ * asking the source to delete) or that moved them already gets a move.
+ *
+ * Copyright 2026 Stained Glass OS contributors
+ *
